Description: Let GNOME Shell menus leave the keyboard focus of the focused window in place.
 GNOME Shell grabs the stage for every menu it opens, and a stage grab moves the Wayland keyboard focus away
 from the focused window. Chromium and Electron derive the active state of a window from its keyboard focus,
 so they deactivate the window, and a click on a menu item of a global menu reaches an inactive window. VS
 Code, for example, drops a menu action which finds no focused window.
 meta_display_set_grab_keeps_keyboard_focus() marks an actor whose stage grabs leave the keyboard focus in
 place. Key events still go only to the grab.
 .
 When such a grab starts, repeating keys the client received as pressed are released for it, because the
 client would not receive their release otherwise and would repeat them. When it ends, the keys the client
 received as pressed and which were released during the grab are released for it, and the modifier state is
 sent again, because key releases and modifier changes during the grab did not reach the client, and Alt of
 Alt+F, which opens a menu, would otherwise stay pressed for the client. During the grab, the input method
 focus leaves the window. Xwayland keeps the usual behavior: X11 clients do not lose their active state when
 Xwayland loses the keyboard focus, and Xwayland releases the keys it holds as pressed only on a leave.
 .
 ClutterStage notifies a new grab-actor property whenever the actor of its topmost grab changes, and the
 keyboard focus is decided again then. Moving from a marked menu to an unmarked one, such as the date menu,
 while the stage stays grabbed, changes the decision.
 .
 The test wayland-keep-keyboard-focus checks that a marked grab sends no leave, releases a held repeating key
 but not a held modifier when it starts, delivers no key events during the grab, and releases the modifier
 released meanwhile and sends the modifier state when it ends,
 that an unmarked grab sends a leave and an enter, and that moving the grab from a marked actor to an
 unmarked one and back sends a leave and an enter.
Author: Mitar
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-24
